# The calculator's grammar layer.
#
# The machine decides what sequence of tokens is legal, and which operator is pending — by
# BEING in the state that names it, not by remembering it in a register. The numbers are
# elsewhere (calculator.cpp): no row can hold one, because a `when` clause compares one int
# that rides on the event (SPEC.md section 3).
#
# Kinds — the format names events by number, so this comment is the name table
# (GENERATOR.md, "Not here yet", item 1):
#   1 Number   value = the operand, 0 .. 2147483647 (never negative: `-` is always the
#              subtraction OPERATOR here, so `0 - 5` rather than `-5`)
#   2 Add    3 Sub    4 Mul    5 Div
#   6 Equals   end of the line: print what is held
#   7 Clear    start of a new line: forget everything
#
# Enter (`Equals`) is deliberately absent from every `Pending*` state: `12 +` is not in this
# machine's language, so the shell says so instead of inventing a value.
version 1
machine Calculator
initial Entry

# The first operand of a line.
transition Entry --1--> Accum action take_operand
transition Entry --7--> Entry action clear_all

# An operator does not need an action to record itself: the state it moves to IS the operator.
transition Accum --2--> PendingAdd
transition Accum --3--> PendingSub
transition Accum --4--> PendingMul
transition Accum --5--> PendingDiv
transition Accum --6--> Accum
# No action on the Equals rows: the shell prints what the registers hold, and an action
# that does nothing is one that should not be named. The state stops where the line
# finished rather than returning to Entry — Clear does that, at the start of the next
# line — so the state a finished line leaves behind says how it ended.
transition Accum --7--> Entry action clear_all

# A number with an operator waiting: the arithmetic runs in the action, which reads the
# operand from the event and the running value from the registers.
transition PendingAdd --1--> Accum action add_operand
transition PendingSub --1--> Accum action sub_operand
transition PendingMul --1--> Accum action mul_operand
transition PendingAdd --7--> Entry action clear_all
transition PendingSub --7--> Entry action clear_all
transition PendingMul --7--> Entry action clear_all

# Division by zero is a ROW, not a register check: the divisor is this event's value and the
# pending operator is a state, so a guard can see what it needs. The generator emits the
# guarded row first, which is what makes `x / 0` reachable at all.
transition PendingDiv --1--> Error when eq 0 action note_division_by_zero
transition PendingDiv --1--> Accum action div_operand
transition PendingDiv --7--> Entry action clear_all

# The error state is TOTAL over the rest of the line on purpose: if a token were refused here
# instead, the shell would report "unexpected operator" and hide the division that caused it.
transition Error --1--> Error
transition Error --2--> Error
transition Error --3--> Error
transition Error --4--> Error
transition Error --5--> Error
transition Error --6--> Error
transition Error --7--> Entry action clear_all